{
  "raw": {
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41375&lang=en": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/26/2026 Posted Date: Oct 12, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM-CHAN Advancement Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Executive Assistant Working Title: Executive Assistant FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range : $72,000 - $78,000 Position #00001288 – Requisition #41375 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . The University of Colorado Denver is a leading public urban research university located in the heart of downtown Denver, where academic excellence, innovation and community partnership come together to expand opportunity and strengthen communities. Within this dynamic institution, the School of Education & Human Development is a statewide and national leader in educational quality, access and equity across the lifespan, from birth through higher education. SEHD prepares equity-minded educators, counselors, researchers and leaders who create meaningful change in schools, families and communities across Colorado and beyond. Through innovative academic programs, impactful research and deep community partnerships, the school advances well-being, opportunity and lifelong learning in diverse urban, rural and global communities. The Director of Development will join SEHD at an important moment of opportunity, building philanthropic partnerships that expand student access, elevate faculty and research, and extend the school’s impact for generations to come. Executive Assistant What you will do: Provide administrative and technical support to the Vice Chancellor for Advancement under the direction of the Strategic Advisor. Coordinate day-to-day activities of the Office of Advancement, handling a broad range of inquiries and assignments. The position possesses project management skills, effective analytical and problem-solving skills, works well under pressure, and can handle complex and concurrent tasks. The position exercises initiative to achieve goals and objectives, maintains confidentiality and establishes and maintains positive working relationships. Key Responsibilities: Provide executive support to the Vice Chancellor of Advancement and members of the Advancement leadership group. Manage Office of Advancement administrative activities/projects. Curates and prioritizes lists of key stakeholders for Chancellor engagement, guiding these lists through multiple layers of internal approval processes to ensure value adds for the Chancellor and campus, and collaborating weekly with the Chancellor’s support staff to schedule and facilitate strategic donor interactions and initiatives. From start to finish, ensure seamless execution of tracking follow-through on action items, preparing detailed briefings and talking points for each interaction, and advising on the structure and content of events and meetings to maximize engagement and alignment with Advancement, Chancellor, and institutional goals. Conduct background & preparation research and compile information for upcoming meetings that include the Vice Chancellor and/or members of the Advancement leadership group. Track and follow up on action items from leadership meetings led by the Vice Chancellor for Advancement and/or by the Strategic Advisor Manage special projects of strategic importance to the Office of Advancement. Manage the Vice Chancellor’s and members of the Advancement leadership group’s calendars, ensuring that engagements are appropriately and sensitively prioritized and managed. Respond in a timely manner to all requests from various sources, both internal and external, for the Vice Chancellor’s time. Coordinate meetings and events that include Vice Chancellor and/or members of the Advancement leadership group. Coordinate departmental agendas, support agenda planning, take notes, and track follow up for meetings that include Vice Chancellor for Advancement. Assist with managing expenses, preparing expense reports, reconciliation, and mileage/travel reports for Vice Chancellor for Advancement under the direction of the Executive Director of Finance & Administration. Provides back-up office presence when needed. Performs all other duties as assigned. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s degree in business, public administration, communications, marketing, social science, or a related field from an accredited institution. 4+ years administrative support experience. Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year-for-year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) At least three (3) years of experience working in a complex university environment with progressive responsibility. Experience with program or project management with ability to effectively lead a team to accomplish critical strategic objectives. Experience with Concur, or similar procurement and travel software, CRM database, & Microsoft Suite. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of, or desire to learn, university policies and procedures, and ability to apply them Strong attention to detail and organizational skills Demonstrate commitment to providing a high level of responsive, thoughtful, and solutions-oriented service to university leadership, colleagues, donors, volunteers, and external partners. Strong communication skills and the ability to work well with a wide audience. Exceptional attention to detail and demonstrate ability to consistently produce accurate, complete work while managing multiple concurrent priorities. Proficiency with communication, word processing, spreadsheet, presentation, and scheduling software (such as Microsoft Outlook, Teams, Word, Excel, Access, PowerPoint, and Adobe Professional). Ability to take dictation. Ability to manage executive email inboxes. Ability to exercise sound judgment, independently prioritize competing and time-sensitive requests, and recognize when an issue requires escalation. Ability to communicate proactively, particularly when priorities change, deadlines may be affected, information is missing or additional clarification is needed. Ability to interact and partner positively with students, faculty, staff, administrators, donors, and visitors. Ability to establish work priorities and adapt to changing needs. Ability to follow through to ensure completion of activities by the deadline established. Ability to communicate effectively with all levels of faculty, administration, staff and students in both a written and verbal capacity. Ability to manage complex, sensitive, and confidential information. Ability to cooperate and support other department staff in order to contribute to a productive work environment. Ability to develop office processes as needed. Ability to work both individually and as a team with the support staff for the Chancellors and other Associate/Vice Chancellors. Other Variables This position will be expected to be in the office four days out of the week. This individual will be provided a remote work day of their choosing. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $72,000-$78,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 26, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications. A current CV/resume. List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to nickolas.chabot-olson@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41348&lang=en": {
      "title": "Treasury Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/17/2026 Posted Date: Oct 12, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: Treasurer Schedule: Full-time Join Our Team and Make a Difference in Higher Education! At the System Administration office at the University of Colorado, we are dedicated to building a workplace where everyone feels valued, supported, and empowered to thrive. Our office is committed to fostering a culture where employees find community, a sense of belonging, and the opportunity to contribute meaningfully to our mission and purpose. By joining our team, you’ll have a direct impact on shaping the future of higher education. In addition to the Office of the Board of Regents, the University of Colorado’s system administration includes 19 units that provide essential services to CU’s campuses in Aurora, Boulder, Colorado Springs, and Denver, as well as the Office of the President. While not a part of any one campus, we are proud to serve in the centralized office for the University system. Together, we ensure CU continues to deliver outstanding education, groundbreaking research, and valuable service to our communities. We believe in creating an environment where the unique experiences and perspectives of everyone are embraced, enriching the work we do and the strategic outcomes we pursue. If you’re passionate about making a difference, contributing to a dynamic team, and supporting a mission that matters, we encourage you to apply and be part of our journey. Learn more about CU System Administration . Position Summary: The Office of the Treasurer is responsible for the University's cash management, debt management, investment oversight, and property management functions. The office provides strategic guidance and support to university campuses and departments regarding financing strategies, banking relationships, real estate matters, faculty housing assistance programs, and treasury-related initiatives. The Treasury Manager serves as a key member of the Office of the Treasurer and is responsible for managing and enhancing the University’s treasury functions in support of the institution’s long-term financial objectives. This position manages debt accounting and reporting activities, supports debt issuance transactions, and ensures compliance with applicable regulatory, contractual, and policy requirements. The person in this position leads and administers projects and programs in areas of debt management and faculty housing assistance program and serves as a resource for programs in other areas of Treasury. The Treasury Manager plays a critical role in providing accurate, timely, and meaningful financial information to internal and external stakeholders, including the Board of Regents, University leadership, rating agencies, and external service providers. The position also supports the effective stewardship of the University's financial resources through the oversight of key Treasury operations, process improvements, and strategic initiatives. The successful candidate will be a collaborative and results-oriented professional with strong analytical, project management, communication, and relationship management skills. The ability to balance strategic thinking with operational execution is essential. This position reports to the Associate Treasurer and is exempt from the State of Colorado Classified Staff System. Where You Will Work: Applicants must be Colorado residents or able to relocate to Colorado within a month of starting employment with University of Colorado System Administration. This role is eligible for a hybrid schedule, working 2-3 days a week in the office at 1800 Grant St, Denver, CO. Please note, leadership may adjust in-office work requirements with a minimum of three months' notice before any change can be implemented. Equal Opportunity Statement: CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Duties and Responsibilities: Duties and responsibilities of the position include, but are not limited to: General Stay informed of capital market developments, treasury management best practices, and emerging financial technologies. Develop a collaborative partnership with campuses, other departments within system administration to identify their needs, address challenges, and implement effective treasury solutions. Take a lead in reviewing and maintaining the University's Debt Management Policy and related procedures. Evaluate the impact of legislative initiatives and regulatory developments affecting Treasury operations and provide analysis regarding potential impacts to the treasury team. Maintain expertise regarding debt-related reporting requirements established by the Board of Regents, federal and state agencies, rating agencies, and other governing bodies. Assist in responding to information requests from the Board of Regents, President's Office, campus CFOs, CCHE, and other internal and external stakeholders. Debt Management Assist in managing and maintaining university’s debt portfolio, including fixed-rate and variable-rate debt, commercial paper programs, bank loans, lines of credit and lease-financing obligations. Assist in analyzing financing alternatives, including public-private partnerships and other innovative financing structures, and prepare recommendations that are consistent with university’s policies and procedures and regulatory requirements. Collaborate with campuses and/or financial advisor and develop financial models and debt affordability analyses to support capital planning and debt capacity assessments. Manage coordination of debt issuance and refinancing transactions, including preparation and organization of bond issuance documents, including Official Statements and other due diligence materials. Lead the collection, validation, and organization of information requested by underwriters, bond and disclosure counsels, financing advisors, rating agencies, auditors and other financing participants. Manage the investment and reinvestment of debt proceeds with the Treasurer and Assistant Treasurer Cash and Short-term Investments. Manage quarterly cash-flow forecasts in collaboration with campuses; analyze funding needs for campus capital projects and inform relevant stakeholders on debt issuance amount/timing, liquidity management and investment decisions. Manage and direct all debt service activities, including monitoring requirements and ensuring the timely fulfillment of principal, interest, and related payment obligations. Develop, maintain, and enhance debt management records and documentation systems. Project and Program Management Lead the implementation, administration, and ongoing optimization of debt management software supporting Treasury operations and reporting. Manage the development, implementation, and administration of the IRC Housing Assistance Program in collaboration with university stakeholders and external partners. Provide oversight and enhance reporting on university’s Faculty Housing Assistance Program (FHAP). Evaluate potential benefit of internal bank structure for capital project financing across the university system and make recommendation if beneficial. Act as a treasury lead in procurement and RFP processes for debt-related service providers. Manage special projects and strategic initiatives assigned by the Treasurer and Associate Treasurer. Reporting and Compliance Prepare and coordinate debt-related reports required by federal regulations, state statutes, Board of Regents policies, rating agencies, debt covenants, contracts, and external service providers. Manage debt compliance calendars and including timely filing of continuing disclosure reports and other required compliance submissions. Manage partnership with campuses and affiliated entities to identify material financial obligations and ensure required notices are submitted through EMMA within regulatory deadlines. Ensure compliance with bond covenants, lease agreements, internal loan agreements, commercial paper programs, variable-rate debt obligations, and other financing arrangements. Manage relationship with the external arbitrage vendor to coordinate rebate calculations and reporting. Accounting and Audit Manage timely recording of debt-related transactions, including issuances, defeasances, amortizations, and other financing activities, in accordance with applicable GASB standards. Provide debt accounting guidance and support to campuses, including project fund allocations and debt-related financial reporting. Perform monthly, quarterly, and annual reconciliations of debt instruments, debt service payments, and project allocations. Manage internal loan activities, including resolution preparation, Board and management approvals, interest-rate determinations, and amortization schedule development. Initiate and/or coordinate debt service payments, wire transfers, and related treasury transactions; reconcile debt balances with trustee statements and general ledger accounts. Partner with the Office of the Controller and external auditors to prepare audit schedules, confirmations, supporting documentation, and follow-up responses. Lead all debt-related audit requests, confirmations, subsequent-event reporting, and supporting documentation requirements Other Duties Contribute in the enhancement of Treasury's cash forecasting, liquidity management, and asset allocation processes as needed. Document debt management and Treasury operational procedures to strengthen internal controls and continuity of operation. Participate in Treasury-related RFP processes and vendor evaluations as needed. Train Treasury staff on debt accounting, reporting, and compliance functions to ensure operational resiliency and adequate backup coverage. Provide backup support for critical Treasury operations as needed. What We Offer: Salary: The anticipated hiring range has been established at $118,000-$124,000. CU System Administration takes into consideration a combination of the selected candidate’s education, training and experience as it relates to the position, as well as the position’s scope and complexity, internal pay equity and external market value when determining a salary level for potential new employees. Benefits: With our unparalleled range of benefits, including top-notch healthcare, comprehensive wellness programs, enriching professional development, and a dynamic work-life balance, we ensure your holistic growth and happiness. For more information on benefits programs, please review our Comprehensive Benefits Guide . Access our Total Compensation Calculator to see what your total rewards could be at CU. (This position is a University Staff position) Qualifications: Minimum Qualifications: Please include all relevant experience in your resume to be accurately assessed against these qualifications. You must meet all minimum requirements at the time of application to be considered for this role. Bachelor's degree in Finance, Accounting, Economics, Business Administration, or a related field from an accredited institution. Five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in treasury, finance, banking, investments, debt management, or a related financial discipline. Three (3) years of experience in debt accounting, debt management, capital financing, or debt compliance activities. One (1) year of experience in a team lead or supervisory role, which includes assigning and reviewing the work of others, providing guidance to internal and external team members, and assisting management with performance reviews (this requirement may be substituted by formal leadership training) Demonstrated experience with debt compliance requirements, including SEC Rule 15c2-12 continuing disclosure obligations. Experience conducting financial analysis and developing financial models. Experience with governmental accounting principles and capital budgeting processes. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree in Finance, Accounting, Business Administration, Public Administration, or a related field. Seven (7) years of professional experience progressively responsible experience in treasury, finance, banking, investments, debt management, or a related financial discipline. Two (2) years of experience leading projects, serving as a team lead, or coordinating and guiding the work of others, including assigning or reviewing work, providing guidance to internal or external team members, and supporting team or organizational priorities. This requirement may be substituted by formal leadership training Experience working in higher education, public finance, municipal finance, or governmental treasury operations. Experience supporting bond issuances, commercial paper programs, variable-rate debt obligations, or other complex financing arrangements. Experience implementing or administering treasury or debt management software platforms. Certified Treasury Professional (CTP), Certified Public Accountant (CPA), Chartered Financial Analyst (CFA), or similar professional designation. Experience with PeopleSoft Financials and debt management systems. Project management experience involving cross-functional stakeholders and enterprise initiatives. How to Apply: For full consideration, please attach the following as separate documents to your application: A cover letter identifying the job specific minimum qualifications you possess and interest in the position. A resume including all relevant experience to be accurately assessed against the qualifications listed in the posting. Please ensure you check the “Job Specific Attachments” box next to each document on the “Required Documents” page of the application for the appropriate documents to be attached. Per the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, you are not required to provide your date of birth, dates of school attendance, or dates of graduation on your resume or cover letter. If an application requires additional materials, such as a transcript or certification, you may redact identifying information relating to your date or birth, dates of school attendance, or dates of graduation. When to Apply: For full consideration completed applications must be submitted by Friday, October 16th . Reference checking is a standard step in our hiring process. You may be asked to provide contact information, including email addresses and phone numbers, for up to five references as part of the search process for this position. *Please note: All application materials must be submitted through CU Careers; emailed materials will not be considered. * Sponsorship Statement: We are unable to sponsor applicants for work visas for this position. Background Check Statement: The University of Colorado is committed to providing a safe and productive learning and living community. To achieve that goal, we conduct background investigations for all final applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial and/or motor vehicle history. The Immigration Reform and Control Act requires that a verification of employment eligibility be documented for all new employees by the end of the third day of work. The University of Colorado is committed to diversity and equality in education and employment. ADA Statement: We are committed to an inclusive and barrier-free search process. We provide accommodations for applicants requesting accommodation through the search process such as alternative formats of this posting. Individuals with disabilities in need of accommodations throughout the search process should contact the ADA Coordinator at: systemhr@cu.edu ."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=38857&lang=en": {
      "title": "Pipe Mechanical Trades - II Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/24/2026 Posted Date: Oct 9, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM AVCFM BMO Anschutz Schedule: Full-time University of Colorado Denver Department: Facilities Management Job Title: Pipe Mechanical Trades - II Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing) Position #: 844879 – Requisition #: 38857 Job Summary: Join the Facilities Management Department on the University of Colorado Denver Campus as a Pipe Mechanical Trades II (PMTII), Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing). This is a full-time position. The PMT-II Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing) position: Maintain all facilities and operating systems in a safe and efficient working order. This includes regular maintenance on HVAC, electrical, mechanical, utility systems, interior finishes, and appliances and coordinate work with journey level technicians and contractors. You will record issues and make work reports via email, log your work in CMMS and monitor building systems in BAS and perform various other duties and tasks as required. This is an evening, second shift position. Hours are Monday through Friday, 9:30am to 6:00pm , and qualify for shift differential. Key Responsibilities: 30% - Zone Preventative Maintenance Receives preventive maintenance work orders, performs preventive maintenance on assigned equipment, which may include the following: faucets, pipes, hot water tanks, toilets, garbage disposals, dishwashers, refrigerators, ceiling lights, and logs reports as required The position must have the ability to perform data entry and be able to operate a computer to log reports. The position will record issues through the shift desk computer and maintain the logbook. The position must have the ability for written communication since the position will maintain the logbook. Set priorities based on urgency and make or arrange for the necessary repairs, including replacing or modifying components (e.g., plumbing, steam, electrical, HVAC, and workspace aesthetic-related items). Evaluate operations and implement recommendations to prevent failures and improve efficiency of HVAC and energy management systems. Maintain and monitor operating logs to determine irregular equipment performance 20% - Corrective Maintenance Respond to corrective maintenance calls and verbal assignments, ensuring that tenant/staff requests are attended to in a timely, courteous, and efficient manner. These work orders include requests for the repair of minor electrical items, including lighting, minor plumbing items, floods, freezer monitoring, HVAC items, including hot/cold calls, and too much/too little air supply. Respond to, and communicate with, tenants to ensure all complaints are resolved in a timely manner and in a craftsman-like fashion. The position will operate a university vehicle to transport necessary tools/equipment to work sites across campus 10% - Project Coordination and Support Assist in Capital Projects as appropriate to ensure quality construction and repairs. Coordinate Facilities Funded Projects within the Zone. Collaborate and advise project managers, general contractors, and vendors during construction, occupancy and warranty phases to ensure all mechanical and control problems are installed/repaired to meet UCD|AMC construction standards. Provide BO&M representation as required. Act as a Subject Matter Expert where appropriate and provide feedback to Project Manager via responses. 10% - Emergencies, Special Events and Other Duties Participates and assists in emergencies based on the magnitude and nature. Supports various special events both in preparation and during the event (i.e. Commencement). Examples of emergencies and special events could include commencement, snow removal, power outages, flood, etc. May be assigned other duties as required. The position must have the ability to communicate with security, fire department, and the police department in case of an emergency and be able to communicate the problem. 30% - Lead Responsibilities Coordinates the team’s tasks, manages procurement, reviews WO entries, and assists team members as needed. Implements leadership priorities to support vision and objectives. Work Location: Onsite – this role is expected to work onsite and is located in Denver, Colorado. Why Join Us: Apply today to be part of a team where the focus is supporting the university’s mission of education the next generation. We are looking for someone with a solid maintenance/service background to learn more and develop new skills. You will be set up for success. We will provide you with the training necessary to become proficient in the equipment we maintain. There are also paid external training opportunities for beneficial technical and professional development courses. All of the hand and specialty tools you need will be provided. We offer robust training program for anyone interested in growing their career options. From hands on work with journey level technicians and online self-paced technical training to paid external trades certifications and CU employee tuition assistance, CU Denver can be the place to boost your career trajectory. Come to a place where you can develop a career, not just another job. For more information about the university of Colorado Denver campus please visit www.ucdenver.edu Why work for the University? We have AMAZING benefits and offer exceptional amounts of holiday, vacation and sick leave! The University of Colorado offers an excellent benefits package including: Medical: Multiple plan options Dental: Multiple plan options Additional Insurance: Disability, Life, Vision Retirement 401(a) Plan: Employer contributes 10% of your gross pay Paid Time Off: Accruals over the year Vacation Days: 22/year (maximum accrual 352 hours) Sick Days: 15/year (unlimited maximum accrual) Holiday Days: 10/year Tuition Benefit: Employees have access to this benefit on all CU campuses ECO Pass: Reduced rate RTD Bus and light rail service There are many additional perks & programs with the CU Advantage . Qualifications: Minimum Qualifications: Three years of progressively responsible residential maintenance experience, including minor repairs to HVAC, electrical, lighting, and plumbing systems and indoor finishes. Colorado law requires that candidates for state government jobs be residents of Colorado at the time of application. Substitution: Successful completion of a formalized plumbing, pipefitting, steam fitting, or heating, ventilation, and air conditioning apprenticeship program or higher level, non-correspondence course of work in the same field from an accredited vocational school or university may substitute for the experience on a year-to-year basis. Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Preferred Qualifications: 1 -3 years of lead experience Experience using computers to monitor buildings tied into Building Automation Systems (BAS) Experience fixing and maintaining equipment and machinery Experience keeping activity logs Experience in heating, ventilation, and air conditioning Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to manage multiple tasks and work independently with minimal supervision. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to establish rapport with people of diverse backgrounds. Experience that demonstrates strong customer service skills. Excellent oral communication skills with the ability to handle stressful situations in a calm and professional manner. Ability to communicate effectively in written form, which includes the ability to check for typographical, grammatical, and spelling errors Mechanical Ability Physical Ability Planning and organization skills. Knowledge of heating, ventilation, and air conditioning How to Apply: For full consideration, please submit the following document(s): 1. A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position (only use if a cover letter is necessary) 2. Curriculum vitae / Resume 3. Three to five professional references, including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address Questions should be directed to: Megan Freije megan.freije@cuanschutz.edu Screening of Applications Begins: Immediately and continues until position is filled. For best consideration, apply by October 23, 2026. Anticipated Pay Range: The starting salary range $4906 - $5690 per month for this position has been established. The second shift differential of 7.5% shall be paid for all hours worked when half or more of the scheduled work hours fall between 4 p.m. and 11 p.m. Monday through Thursday. The above salary range ( or hiring range ) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position may be eligible for overtime compensation, depending on the level. This position may be eligible for a one-time non-base building sign-on bonus up to $2,000. Details of this recruitment incentive program will be provided during the interview process. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Total Compensation Calculator Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. ADA Statement: The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at hr.adacoordinator@cuanschutz.edu ​. Background Check Statement: The University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus is dedicated to ensuring a safe and secure environment for our faculty, staff, students and visitors. To assist in achieving that goal, we conduct background investigations for all prospective employees. Vaccination Statement: CU Anschutz strongly encourages vaccination against the COVID-19 virus and other vaccine preventable diseases . If you work, visit, or volunteer in healthcare facilities or clinics operated by our affiliated hospital or clinical partners or by CU Anschutz, you will be required to comply with the vaccination and medical surveillance policies of the facilities or clinics where you work, visit, or volunteer, respectively. In addition, if you work in certain research areas or perform certain safety sensitive job duties, you must enroll in the occupational health medical surveillance program ."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41337&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Budget Director",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/01/2026 Posted Date: Oct 8, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM-AVCBO Budget Office-Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Business Services Assistant Director Working Title: Assistant Budget Director FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $102,197 - $112,955 | Position #00804189 – Requisition #41337 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Assistant Budget Director provides budget and financial services for the University of Colorado Denver by coordinating the request and operating budget cycles in an efficient and timely manner. In this role, the Assistant Budget Director will work with many constituencies including campus administration, campus operating units, employee groups, and the President’s Office. Assistant Budget Director What you will do: Supervises team of three Budget Analysts engaged in providing budget support for all administrative units and the Denver schools & colleges, including preparing annual budget requests, regular budget analysis, and revisions. Assist in reconciling and implementing the annual CU Denver operating budget in collaboration with the Anschutz team. Collaborate with Denver School and College fiscal managers with developing and maintaining budgets. Identify inefficient processes and make recommendations for improvement. Preparation of the annual University of Colorado Denver Budget Data document for state General Fund support by gathering data in accordance with state, CCHE, and University guidelines and specific formats. Develops related Budget Data schedules on data and information for student enrollment, tuition, and other programs. Monitor the budgetary data maintained in the University financial reporting system by reviewing departmental changes to initial operating budgets for reasonable budgeting decisions, compliance with applicable guidelines, and proper preparation. Assists in responding to requests for information from the President’s Office, auditors, Administration and outside agencies. Manage processes in Anaplan through verifying system data, preparing drivers, determining Denver Campus security, creating reports, and managing documentation and training. Oversee the communications and change management for Anaplan including ensuring the distribution of information to end users on system design, updates, training, and deadlines. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. A bachelor’s degree in business, business administration, finance, accounting, or directly related field from an accredited institution. Four years of progressively responsible finance or budget related experience, with at least one year in higher education or state government. The position requires extensive, diverse and progressively responsible experience, including direct responsibility for higher education budgets. Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Experience supervising a team of analysts. PeopleSoft or other Oracle-based financial system experience is highly desired. Anaplan based budgeting system experience is highly desired. Experience with the State of Colorado and/or University of Colorado budgetary processes is highly desired. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Leadership and team development skills. Advanced knowledge of fiscal management and HR practices. Ability to influence institutional financial strategy. Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Outstanding customer service skills. Demonstrate an exceptional understanding of access and engagement with a commitment to developing equitable practices. Knowledge of accounting and budgeting processes. Ability to evaluate existing processes and recommend improvements. Demonstrated competence in the use of MS Office applications, particularly MS Excel and MS Access, for spreadsheets, statistical analysis, graphics, and decision support. Analytical skills including the ability to apply math (algebra) skills, analyze implications of changes in methods, input data, policies, etc. Able to analyze and interpret financial data by using spreadsheets, databases, and modeling applications. Understanding of higher education policies and issues. Understanding of relational database concepts and queries. Strong organizational and time management skills; ability to meet multiple deadlines and manage interrelated and/or complex issues or transactions. Ability to evaluate policies and exercise good judgment in the interpretation and application of procedures and rules. Conditions of Employment This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $102,197 - $112,955. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 22, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Tawnya Boulier, tawnya.boulier@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41323&lang=en": {
      "title": "Associate Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/01/2026 Posted Date: Oct 8, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: VCSSLS-AVCSCE UnivAdv&StdEngag Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Associate Vice Chancellor Working Title: Associate Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $200,000-$220,000 Position #00350581 – Requisition #41323 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Associate Vice Chancellor (AVC) for Student Affairs provides strategic and visionary leadership for a comprehensive, student-centered approach to engagement, belonging, wellbeing, and success. The AVC establishes the vision, priorities, strategies, and organizational infrastructure necessary to create an exceptional student experience and ensure students have the support and resources they need to thrive. As a key leader within the Division of Strategic Enrollment and Student Success (SESS), the AVC reports to the Vice Chancellor for Strategic Enrollment and Student Success and serves as a strategic partner to senior leaders across the university. The AVC leads student services and resources that promote personal development, student engagement, wellbeing, and a safe, welcoming, and inclusive campus community. Through these efforts, Student Affairs plays a critical role in advancing student persistence, retention, completion, and overall success. The AVC will bring a deep understanding of the opportunities and challenges facing today’s higher education institutions, particularly those affecting an increasingly diverse student population. The AVC will use data, research, assessment, and student feedback to identify emerging needs, inform decisions, evaluate effectiveness, and address structural barriers that may impede student success. The AVC will also build strong relationships across academic and administrative units and with external partners to strengthen the university’s capacity to serve students effectively. The AVC will be a forward-thinking and entrepreneurial leader who anticipates the evolving needs of students and develops innovative approaches to the student experience. The AVC will strategically prioritize people, programs, technology, and financial resources to maximize student success and institutional impact. The position provides leadership for professional and student staff, has fiscal responsibility for the Student Affairs portfolio and its operating budgets, and leads organizational strategies that promote a high-performing, collaborative, and student-centered culture. As a member of the SESS leadership team, the AVC serves as a campus-wide leader and strategic partner at the intersection of academics, enrollment, student success, wellbeing, and external engagement. The AVC will work across organizational boundaries to align resources, strengthen partnerships, remove barriers, and advance institutional priorities that enable every student to succeed. Associate Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs What you will do: Develop and lead a comprehensive Student Affairs strategy aligned with the university’s mission, strategic plan, enrollment goals, and student success priorities. Translate institutional priorities into measurable goals, strategies, initiatives, and outcomes. Serve as a strategic advisor to senior leadership, including the Senior Vice Chancellor for Strategic Enrollment and Student Success (SESS) and the SESS leadership team, on matters related to the student experience, engagement, campus climate, wellbeing, safety, and retention. Collaborate effectively with deans, Academic Affairs, and campus and external partners. Provide visionary leadership for the student experience, creating a welcoming, inclusive, engaging, and student-centered campus environment. Strengthen programs and services that advance belonging, community, leadership development, engagement, personal growth, wellbeing, and student success. Lead strategic planning, organizational development, and continuous improvement across the Student Affairs portfolio. Streamline functions, processes, practices, and technology to improve service delivery, operational effectiveness, student outcomes, and retention. Use data, research, assessment, and student feedback to identify emerging needs, evaluate programs and services, inform decision-making, and ensure student experiences meaningfully shape institutional policies and priorities. Promote a culture that recognizes students as partners in shaping the university experience. Lead a coordinated student success continuum that advances engagement, retention, wellbeing, and student success through collaboration across CU Denver, the tri-institutional community, AHEC, and external partners. Provide strategic leadership for student wellbeing, mental health, wellness, and conduct, working collaboratively across disciplines to strengthen a proactive campus wellness culture that supports student success. Lead and develop the Student Affairs leadership team, providing supervision, coaching, performance management, professional development, and support for six direct reports and the broader Student Affairs workforce. Foster a collaborative, supportive, high-performing workplace. Provide executive oversight of fiscal and administrative operations, including budget planning, resource allocation, business management, housing assets, contract services, negotiations, and procurement in accordance with university policies and procedures. Leverage internal and external resources and partnerships to strengthen the campus community, enhance the student experience, advance institutional priorities, and elevate the university’s reputation. Travel as needed to support university, community, and professional responsibilities. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Earned Master’s degree in higher education, educational leadership, business administration, public administration or a closely related field Eight (8) years of diverse and progressively responsible experience at the management level, including direct responsibility for student services and/or related services in higher education. Progressive responsibility and experience in supervising professional and administrative staff. Demonstrable experience in managing and working with complex budgets. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Earned doctorate Student Affairs and student success experience at a higher education institution that is a comprehensive urban campus. Documented experience in developing, implementing and evaluating unit budgets, goals and strategic planning. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong understanding of university student services and development programs, policies, and practices. Ability to work effectively with diverse teams and individuals and units on and off campus.\\ Demonstrated leadership, budget management and interpersonal skills. A leadership style that promotes collaboration and cooperation. Commitment to excellence, creativity and innovation. Strong oral and written communication skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and maintain interpersonal relationships within an ethnically and culturally diverse urban learning environment with a commitment to inclusion, diversity and respect. Documented experience in developing, implementing and evaluating unit goals and strategic planning. Computer proficiency including MS Office, social media and CRM experience. Ability to prioritize and perform multiple projects simultaneously. Eagerness to acquire new skills. Dependable, team-oriented and a self-starter. Ability to manage and coach team members and develop staff. Skilled record of accomplishment leading, supervising, evaluating, hiring and resolving staff performance problems. Ability to think strategically and aptitude for managing multiple tasks and priorities. Experience collaborating with multiple stakeholders. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $200,000 - $220,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by October 31st. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Tyler Keyworth, tyler.keyworth@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41344&lang=en": {
      "title": "Faculty Fellow for Lecturer Success",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/28/2026 Posted Date: Oct 7, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: AVCASA-FAUE FacAffrs/UG Enrich Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Faculty Fellow for Lecturer Success Working Title: Faculty Fellow FTE: Part-time Salary Range: $5,000 stipend Position #00853662– Requisition #41344 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Center for Faculty Development & Advancement (CFDA) seeks a faculty fellow for the 26-27 academic year to support initiatives that enhance the success, engagement, and professional development of lecturers across CU Denver. Lecturers play a critical role in advancing the university's mission through high-quality teaching, student mentorship, and contributions to student success, retention, and belonging. This fellowship provides an opportunity for a faculty member to lead efforts that strengthen community, recognition, professional growth, and institutional connection among lecturers. A primary responsibility of this fellow will be the planning and implementation of the inaugural Lecturer Summit, a spring 2027 collaborative event sponsored by CFDA and UCDALI that celebrates and supports lecturers through professional learning, networking, and community-building opportunities. In partnership with CFDA and campus stakeholders, the fellow will have substantial autonomy to shape the vision, content, and experience of the summit while developing additional initiatives that support lecturer success throughout the academic year. This position is ideal for a faculty member who is passionate about teaching excellence, faculty development, community building, and advancing institutional support for lecturers. This position requires an estimated commitment of 5-7 hours per week during the academic year. Faculty Fellow for Lecturer Success What you will do: Lecturer Summit Leadership Serve as the primary faculty lead and organizer for the inaugural Lecturer Summit. Collaborate with CFDA and UCDALI, Bridge Liaisons, and other campus partners to develop the summit theme, goals, learning outcomes, and program structure. Compile data from lecturers around needs and desires for the Lecturer Summit learning outcomes. Design engaging programming focused on topics such as: Effective and innovative teaching practices Student success and engagement Faculty well-being and resilience Career development pathways for lecturers Inclusive and equitable teaching practices Cross-disciplinary collaboration and networking Identify and recruit speakers, facilitators, and panelists. Facilitate summit sessions and discussions as appropriate. Recruit collaborators to increase scope and impact of Summit. Assist with participant marketing, outreach, and promotion. Evaluate the effectiveness of the Lecturer Summit. Lecturer Community Building Develop opportunities for networking and community among lecturers across schools and colleges. Gather feedback from lecturers regarding professional development needs and institutional support. Foster cross-disciplinary connections and sharing of effective teaching practices. Program Development and Innovation Work collaboratively with CFDA staff to develop new programming and resources that support lecturer success. Recommend strategies for increasing lecturer engagement, recognition, and professional growth opportunities. Develop and pilot creative initiatives aligned with identified lecturer needs and interests. Assessment and Continuous Improvement Collect and analyze participant feedback from the Lecturer Summit and related programming. Prepare a brief annual report summarizing outcomes, recommendations, and future opportunities. Identify metrics and indicators of success related to lecturer engagement and satisfaction. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Full-time faculty member at CU Denver. Demonstrated commitment to teaching excellence and student success. Strong interpersonal, communication, and collaboration skills. Interest in faculty development, mentoring, and community building. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Experience designing or facilitating professional development programs. Experience organizing events, workshops, learning communities, or conferences. Experience supporting lecturer and/or IRC faculty development. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive excellence and belonging. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Passionate about supporting faculty success and professional growth. Bring creativity, initiative, and innovative thinking to program development. Enjoy designing and facilitating engaging learning experiences. Demonstrate strong organizational skills. Value collaboration, inclusion, and community building. Be committed to helping faculty thrive throughout their academic careers. Conditions of Employment This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. • Current faculty appointment at the University of Colorado Denver. • Position will last for one academic year Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at - $5,000 stipend. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is/is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 28, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to Aubrey Thorburn, AUBREY.THORBURN@UCDENVER.EDU . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41335&lang=en": {
      "title": "Professor, Director of Construction",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/31/2026 Posted Date: Oct 6, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CEDC-Civil Engineering Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Professor, Faculty and Director Working Title: Professor, Director of Construction FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $85,000.00 - $130,000.00, 9-month contract Position #: 00350978 - Req #41335 About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. About the College The College of Engineering, Design and Computing (CEDC) takes pride in having a faculty of excellent scholars and engineering practitioners. We attract hard-working students who are enthusiastic about engineering, and we are redesigning engineering education to create the agile versatile engineers of the future. Providing an education of enduring value to our students is at the heart of our College mission. We integrate design innovation and computing technology across disciplines, in conjunction with authentic experiences that develop human and social skills such as creativity, collaboration, entrepreneurship, and leadership. We embrace and leverage our setting across urban and medical campuses to broadly impact the social and economic growth of the Denver urban corridor through enterprise partnerships. Our goal is to emerge as Denver’s technological innovation engine and significantly impact Colorado, the nation, and the world via informatics, infrastructure, sustainability, as a smart city. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Department of Civil Engineering and Construction in the College of Engineering, Design and Computing at the University of Colorado Denver invites applications for the position of Director of Construction. We seek an accomplished leader in construction education, research, and/or professional practice to lead CU Denver’s growing Construction Management and Construction Engineering Management programs. The successful candidate will provide strategic leadership for the programs, support and mentor faculty and students, strengthen industry partnerships, and guide continued growth in education, research, and external engagement. We welcome candidates whose careers bridge academia and professional practice, including candidates with substantial leadership experience in the Architecture, Engineering, and Construction (AEC) industry. Candidates do not need to currently hold a tenured faculty appointment to be considered. The successful candidate will receive a faculty appointment in the Department of Civil Engineering and Construction and a separate four-year appointment as Director of Construction. Depending on the candidate’s background and qualifications, the faculty appointment may be as a tenured Associate Professor or Professor or on the Teaching Professor track. Teaching duties will be in construction engineering management, with upcoming needs in Construction Management Fundamentals, Field Engineering and Management, Materials and Methods, Advanced Construction Technology, Construction Safety, and Construction Engineering. Faculty track and rank will be commensurate with the candidate’s education, experience, qualifications, and career path. Why CU Denver and Denver CU Denver's downtown campus places faculty in the heart of a major metropolitan region, with close connections to construction firms, public agencies, professional organizations, and industry partners across Colorado. Denver offers the cultural and professional resources of a major city, with the Rocky Mountains and extensive outdoor recreation nearby. CU Denver combines the resources of the University of Colorado system with an urban campus closely connected to the community it serves. Eligible employees have access to health and retirement benefits, tuition assistance, professional development resources, and RTD EcoPass transit benefits. About the Department The Civil Engineering and Construction department has 10 full-time tenured and tenure-track faculty, 3 teaching-track faculty, and many part-time lecturers. The department offers undergraduate and graduate degrees, including master's and doctoral degrees, in civil engineering, construction engineering management, and construction management. The civil engineering program has been accredited continuously since its inception, and the newer construction programs were first accredited in 2023. In 2025, the department had 75 graduate students and 310 undergraduate students. Approximately 35% are in the construction programs. About the Program The Construction Engineering and Management (CEM) program at the University of Colorado Denver (CU Denver) launched in Fall 2014 as a graduate specialty of the Master of Engineering in Civil Engineering in response to local industry requests and support. Work on two bachelor's degrees began in 2017: the Bachelor of Science (BS) in Construction Management (CM) and the Bachelor of Science in Construction Engineering Management (CEM). CEDC moved forward with both degrees because of interdisciplinary support throughout the College and across CU Denver, especially from the College of Architecture and Planning and the Business School. The two BS degrees were approved by CU Denver, the University of Colorado Regents, and the Colorado Department of Higher Education in Fall 2019, launched in August 2020, and accredited by ABET in 2023. Professor, Director of Construction What you will do: The faculty responsibilities associated with this position depend on the appointment track and reflect the workload distribution while serving as Director of Construction. A Teaching Professor-track appointment will include 40% teaching and 60% service, while a tenure-line appointment will include 20% teaching, 20% research, and 60% service. The 60% service allocation includes the leadership and administrative responsibilities associated with the four-year Director appointment. The Director appointment includes an additional academic year administrative stipend equal to 7–10% of the faculty base salary. If the Director appointment ends, the faculty workload would be adjusted consistent with the individual’s faculty track and other assigned responsibilities. Lead the Construction Programs Recruit, develop, and retain outstanding faculty and staff Oversee program ABET accreditation; manage operations and budget in partnership with department leadership; lead issue resolution Grow Enrollment and Program Reach Lead strategy to grow and retain undergraduate and graduate enrollment Increase the program’s visibility and reputation regionally and nationally Advance Academic Quality and Student Experience Champion impactful curricular and extracurricular learning opportunities Integrate emerging construction technologies into teaching and learning Strengthen Industry Engagement and Fundraising Continue to build and activate a strong Industry Advisory Board (IAB) to support program relevance, student opportunities, and resource development Diversify program revenue streams Strengthen Research and External Funding Support growth in research activity and external funding across the program Support and mentor faculty in developing research and scholarly activities Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. All candidates: Demonstrated leadership experience in academia, the construction industry, or a closely related setting. For appointment on the tenure line: PhD in Construction Management, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, or a related field. A proven record of teaching, research, and service accomplishments commensurate with unit-level and college requirements for appointment with tenure at the Associate Professor or Professor level in the Department of Civil Engineering and Construction. For appointment on the Teaching Professor track: Terminal degree in Construction Management, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, or a related field, or equivalent qualifications and professional experience consistent with the applicable Teaching Professor-track appointment. A proven record of teaching and/or professional accomplishments commensurate with unit-level and college requirements for appointment at the applicable rank on the Teaching Professor track in the Department of Civil Engineering and Construction. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Competitive candidates may demonstrate strengths in several of the following areas. Candidates are not expected to possess every preferred qualification. Professional experience in or substantial engagement with the Architecture, Engineering, and Construction (AEC) industry. Experience developing industry partnerships, working with advisory boards, and/or supporting fundraising or external engagement. Experience teaching, training, advising, or mentoring students or professionals in construction-related fields. Experience with accreditation, program assessment, or continuous improvement; ABET experience is preferred. Interest in or experience with emerging construction technologies, including applications of AI in education, research, or professional practice. Professional Engineer licensure or other relevant professional license or certification. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Effective communication, interpersonal, and relationship-building skills. Ability to lead collaboratively and support the professional growth of faculty and staff. Strong commitment to undergraduate and graduate education and student success across a range of backgrounds, educational pathways, and career goals. Ability to guide curriculum and program development in response to changes in construction education and professional practice. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. Working from the office is encouraged particularly when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements The ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position may be eligible now and in the future for certain visa sponsorship limited to Change of Status (COS) within the United States or if the candidate meets the criteria for the portability provision. While the hiring unit is willing to pursue visa sponsorship, an offer of employment will be contingent on the candidate having a visa status that allows employment with the University as of the start date for the position or the University’s petition being approved and on the candidate being able to obtain the appropriate visa status. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at: In addition to the faculty salary listed below, the four-year Director appointment includes an administrative stipend equal to 7–10% of the faculty base salary. Professor: $120,000 - $130,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. Associate Professor: $110,000 - $115,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. Teaching Professor-track appointment: $95,000 - $105,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. Teaching Associate Professor-track appointment: $85,000 - $95,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by January 1, 2027. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Interested applicants are welcome to continue to apply. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A one-page cover letter describing interest in the Director of Construction role and relevant qualifications. A current CV/resume. A two-page director philosophy. The names and contact information of three professional references. Candidates will be notified before references are contacted. Questions should be directed to David Mays, David.Mays@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41299&lang=en": {
      "title": "Director of Development SEHD",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/15/2026 Posted Date: Oct 2, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM-CHAN Advancement Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Development Senior Professional Working Title: Director of Development (SEHD) FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $90,000-$97,000 Position #00300024 – Requisition #41299 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . *This is a term limited position that is currently funded through June 30th 2029.* The position will be reviewed by January 2029 to assess financial sustainability. If your term of employment will be concluded based on a lack of funding, you will be provided 30 days’ notice of the term of employment in writing by the University. Director of Development (SEHD) The University of Colorado Denver is a leading public urban research university located in the heart of downtown Denver, where academic excellence, innovation and community partnership come together to expand opportunity and strengthen communities. Within this dynamic institution, the School of Education & Human Development is a statewide and national leader in educational quality, access and equity across the lifespan, from birth through higher education. SEHD prepares equity-minded educators, counselors, researchers and leaders who create meaningful change in schools, families and communities across Colorado and beyond. Through innovative academic programs, impactful research and deep community partnerships, the school advances well-being, opportunity and lifelong learning in diverse urban, rural and global communities. The Director of Development will join SEHD at an important moment of opportunity, building philanthropic partnerships that expand student access, elevate faculty and research, and extend the school’s impact for generations to come. What you will do: Job Purpose: The Director of Development is responsible for leading, developing and executing advancement efforts, which includes developing and managing relationships with gift prospects, and soliciting and stewarding major gifts to maximize philanthropic support for the School of Education & Human Development. Serves as a philanthropic advisor and builds relationships within the School of Education & Human Development to facilitate meaningful connections for donors. Impact & Influence: In a centralized Advancement structure with a team of 23 professionals, the Director of Development is a major gift officer who will grow the pipeline of major and principal gift prospects and donors by partnering with university administration, the Vice Chancellor for Advancement, Executive Director, Deans, faculty, and other campus partners and collaborate with Donor Engagement, Advancement Communications, Alumni Engagement, Gift Planning, fellow major gift officers, and colleagues in central Advancement, including Research, Strategy, and Prospect Management. Success in this role requires strong collaboration, strategic management of donor relationships, accountability for fundraising results, and a commitment to ethical, donor-centered fundraising practices. Key Responsibilities - 80% Maximize the philanthropic potential of a portfolio of major gift prospects by soliciting and closing gifts that advance the mission of the university with a portfolio heavily populated but not limited to Alumni, friends, corporations and foundations. The portfolio will vacillate based on the rating and research analysis of the best targets for major gift opportunities in service to the university. Qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward a portfolio of 80-100 prospects, including individuals, corporations, and foundations (SEHD has a long history of heavy corporate, foundation, and gift planning engagement) for gifts of $25,000+. Align donor interests with the strategic priorities of the university. Analyze prospect research materials to target likely donors of $25,000+. Ensure that contact reports are written and entered in a timely manner and utilized to gain the maximum value from future donor engagements. Work with Executive Director of Development on developing an annual fundraising operating plan to establish fundraising goals built on portfolio projections and historical fundraising. Build fundraising strategies around existing research activities, specifically interdisciplinary societal grand challenges identified by the university. In addition to fundraising goals, the Director of Development will be responsible for meeting specific process targets designed to ensure a disciplined approach to building a successful major gift program, such as visits, proposals, and portfolio penetration. Serve as an Advancement liaison to campus units - 20% Creating and implementing fundraising strategies for programs and initiatives that focus on rated targets of $25,000+ Developing familiarity with campus and unit fundraising priorities Working collaboratively with campus partners and Advancement colleagues to formulate plans for prospect engagement and identifying potential top donors not yet aware of or engaged with the university Facilitating interaction between major gift prospects and campus and system leaders and faculty other duties as assigned. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s Degree in nonprofit management, business, marketing or other related field Two (2) years of experience in fundraising, alumni/donor relations, or other nonprofit role or equivalent experience in for-profit marketing or project management or directly related field Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Master’s Degree in a directly related field. Five (5) or more years of professional related experience in fundraising in a college or university development program or other comparable charitable organization. Experience managing high-level volunteers. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of major gift fundraising within a higher education or similar environment. Superior relationship building skills. Proven success in securing and conducting face to face meetings for major solicitations. Excellent spoken and written communication skills. Ability to prioritize, negotiate, and influence effectively. Effective interpersonal, strategic planning, administrative and organizational skills. Solid judgement and sensitivity to others. Ability to work independently and as part of a larger team. Familiarity with financial planning, tax and charitable gift laws, and planned giving vehicles and methods. Ability to effectively manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to cold call. Ability to qualify and disqualify. Ability to work with a generalized prospect pool. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and internet navigation tools with proven knowledge of word processing, spreadsheet software, email, internet access software, and CRM. Ability to meet metrics and establish goals. Maintain excellent records and stay up to date with contact reports, expense reports, and all required internal administrative processes. Proven track record of donor engagement to include identification, cultivation, solicitation at > $25,000. Ability to create individual donor strategies for solicitation. Demonstrates commitment to ethical fundraising practices and effective engagement with various constituencies. Other Variables: Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. This position requires great flexibility around weekend and evening activities. Shows enthusiasm for urban public higher education mission. Must have legal driver’s license. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $90,000-$97,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 16, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: • A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications • A current CV/resume • List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to nickolas.chabot-olson@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41297&lang=en": {
      "title": "Director of Development - CAM",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/15/2026 Posted Date: Oct 2, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM-CHAN Advancement Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Development Senior Professional Working Title: Director of Development (CAM) FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $90,000-$97,000 Position #000001242 – Requisition #41297 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . *This is a term limited position that is currently funded through June 30th 2029.* The position will be reviewed by January 2029 to assess financial sustainability. If your term of employment will be concluded based on a lack of funding, you will be provided 30 days’ notice of the term of employment in writing by the University. Director of Development (CAM) The University of Colorado Denver is Colorado’s public urban research university, located in the heart of a city known for creativity, innovation and cultural vitality. Within this dynamic environment, the College of Arts & Media (CAM) is home to an ambitious community of artists, makers, scholars and innovators preparing to shape the rapidly evolving creative industries. Through nationally recognized programs in film and television, music and entertainment, visual arts, recording arts and media forensics, CAM combines rigorous academic study with hands-on creative practice, state-of-the-art technology and meaningful connections to Denver’s thriving arts and entertainment community. The Director of Development will join CAM at an exciting moment of possibility, building philanthropic partnerships that expand access for talented students, faculty and creative excellence, strengthen industry and community engagement, and bring bold artistic ideas to life. What you will do: Job Purpose: The Director of Development (CAM) is responsible for leading, developing and executing advancement efforts, which includes developing and managing relationships with gift prospects, and soliciting and stewarding major gifts to maximize philanthropic support for the College of Arts & Media. Serves as a philanthropic advisor and builds relationships within the College of Arts & Media to facilitate meaningful connections for donors. Impact & Influence: In a centralized Advancement structure with a team of 23 professionals, the Director of Development is a major gift officer who will grow the pipeline of major and principal gift prospects and donors by partnering with university administration, the Vice Chancellor for Advancement, Executive Director, Deans, faculty, and other campus partners and collaborate with Donor Engagement, Advancement Communications, Alumni Engagement, Gift Planning, fellow major gift officers, and colleagues in central Advancement, including Research, Strategy, and Prospect Management. Success in this role requires strong collaboration, strategic management of donor relationships, accountability for fundraising results, and a commitment to ethical, donor-centered fundraising practices. Key Responsibilities - 80% Maximize the philanthropic potential of a portfolio of major gift prospects by soliciting and closing gifts that advance the mission of the university with a portfolio heavily populated but not limited to Alumni, friends, corporations and foundations. The portfolio will vacillate based on the rating and research analysis of the best targets for major gift opportunities in service to the university. Qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward a portfolio of 80-100 prospects, including individuals, corporations, and foundations, for gifts of $25,000+. Align donor interests with the strategic priorities of the university. Analyze prospect research materials to target likely donors of $25,000+. Ensure that contact reports are written and entered in a timely manner and utilized to gain the maximum value from future donor engagements. Work with Executive Director on developing an annual fundraising operating plan to establish fundraising goals built on portfolio projections and historical fundraising. Build fundraising strategies around existing research activities, specifically interdisciplinary societal grand challenges identified by the university. In addition to fundraising goals, the Director of Development will be responsible for meeting specific process targets designed to ensure a disciplined approach to building a successful major gift program, such as visits, proposals, and portfolio implementation. Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. This position requires great flexibility around weekend and evening activities. Serve as an Advancement liaison to campus units - 20% Creating and implementing fundraising strategies for programs and initiatives that focus on rated targets of $25,000+. Developing familiarity with campus and unit fundraising priorities. Working collaboratively with campus partners and Advancement colleagues to formulate plans for prospect engagement and identifying potential top donors not yet aware of or engaged with the university. Facilitating interaction between major gift prospects and campus and system leaders and faculty. Other duties as assigned. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s Degree in nonprofit management, business, marketing or other related field. Two (2) years of experience in fundraising, alumni/donor relations, or other nonprofit role or equivalent experience in for-profit marketing or project management or directly related field. Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Master’s Degree in a directly related field. Five (5) or more years of professional related experience in fundraising in a college or university development program or other comparable charitable organization. Experience managing high-level volunteers. Familiarity with the arts community, including visual and multi-media. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of major gift fundraising within a higher education or similar environment. Superior relationship building skills. . Proven success in securing and conducting face to face meetings for major solicitations . Excellent spoken and written communication skills. Ability to prioritize, negotiate, and influence effectively. Effective interpersonal, strategic planning, administrative and organizational skills. Solid judgement and sensitivity to others. Ability to work independently and as part of a larger team. Familiarity with financial planning, tax and charitable gift laws, and planned giving vehicles and methods. Ability to effectively manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to cold call. Ability to qualify and disqualify. Ability to work with a generalized prospect pool. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and internet navigation tools with proven knowledge of word processing, spreadsheet software, email, internet access software, and CRM. Ability to meet metrics and establish goals. Maintain excellent records and stay up to date with contact reports, expense reports, and all required internal administrative processes. Proven track record of donor engagement to include identification, cultivation, solicitation of more than $25,000. Ability to create individual donor strategies for solicitation. Demonstrates commitment to ethical fundraising practices and effective engagement with various constituencies. Other Variables: Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. This position requires great flexibility around weekend and evening activities. Shows enthusiasm for urban public higher education mission. Must have legal driver’s license. Conditions of Employment This is a term limited position that is currently funded through June 30th 2029. Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $90,000-$97,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 16, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to nickolas.chabot-olson@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
